(D,"mcq",
"25. A pressure vessel safety device inspection finds that the PRV discharge pipe contains a removable plug (blind cap) that was installed during construction and was never removed. The PRV system is fully operational with the plug in place, and recent testing showed the PRV opened at 100 bar (set pressure). What is the concern?",
"No concern — the PRV opened correctly at set pressure during testing; the discharge plug does not affect set pressure.\nMajor safety concern — the plug on the discharge piping creates back pressure on the PRV; more critically, if the PRV opens during an overpressure event, the plug prevents relief gas from discharging safely and could cause catastrophic failure of the discharge pipe or PRV body.\nMinor concern — remove the plug before commissioning; the PRV operation is not affected during bench testing.\nNo concern — all PRV discharge pipes require temporary plugs during construction to prevent debris ingress.",
"Major safety concern — the plug on the discharge piping creates back pressure on the PRV; more critically, if the PRV opens during an overpressure event, the plug prevents relief gas from discharging safely and could cause catastrophic failure of the discharge pipe or PRV body.",
"A plugged PRV discharge pipe is an extremely dangerous pre-commissioning failure — it is the most common catastrophic mechanism for PRV system failure during an overpressure event. The PRV may open but the pressurised fluid cannot escape, creating a closed system at relief pressure. This is a critical safety finding requiring immediate stop-work and removal of the plug before any pressurisation of the vessel.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"26. During a pressure vessel hydrostatic test, cracks are heard coming from within the vessel during pressure hold at 85% of test pressure. The crew wants to continue pressurising to full test pressure. What is the correct action?",
"Continue — stress relief cracking sounds are normal during initial pressurisation and will cease at full test pressure.\nImmediately stop pressurisation, safely vent the vessel, and investigate the cracking sound source before any further pressurisation.\nContinue pressurisation more slowly to reduce the rate of loading.\nStop pressurisation and add a dye penetrant tracer to the test water to locate the source of cracking.",
"Immediately stop pressurisation, safely vent the vessel, and investigate the cracking sound source before any further pressurisation.",
"Audible cracking during pressure testing is never acceptable — it indicates crack propagation or joint failure in progress. Continuing to full test pressure under these conditions risks catastrophic brittle fracture of the vessel. The vessel must be safely depressurised and thoroughly investigated (VT, PT, UT) before any further pressurisation attempt.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"27. A gearbox oil analysis from a newly commissioned compressor drive shows a metallic particle count of 1,200 particles/ml (diameter >5 μm). The baseline limit in the equipment specification is 500 particles/ml for a new unit at initial commissioning. What is the assessment?",
"Accept — high particle counts are normal during initial break-in; re-test after 200 hours of operation.\nStop the equipment — particle count 2.4× the specification limit indicates accelerated internal wear; investigate the source (bearing, gear tooth, seal) before continuing operation.\nAccept — ISO oil cleanliness requirements are only applicable to hydraulic systems, not gearbox lubricating oil.\nAccept — re-sample from a different drain point; the result may reflect sampling contamination.",
"Stop the equipment — particle count 2.4× the specification limit indicates accelerated internal wear; investigate the source (bearing, gear tooth, seal) before continuing operation.",
"Elevated metallic particle count in a new gearbox is a serious warning sign. While some break-in wear is expected, 2.4× the specified baseline limit indicates abnormal internal wear. The source must be identified before the gearbox can safely continue operation. Ignoring elevated particle counts at commissioning leads to premature catastrophic failure.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"28. A pressure vessel repair per ASME PCC-2 Article 2.1 uses weld overlay to restore wall thickness in a corroded area. The repair must be inspected. Which of the following is the correct NDE sequence for the weld overlay repair?",
"VT after welding → RT after PWHT → Final VT before release.\nMT or PT of base metal before welding → VT during welding (inter-pass) → PT or MT after final pass → UT after PWHT to confirm thickness restoration → Final VT.\nUT of base metal before welding → VT during welding → UT after PWHT → Final VT before release.\nPT before welding → RT during welding → Hardness testing after PWHT → Final RT.",
"MT or PT of base metal before welding → VT during welding (inter-pass) → PT or MT after final pass → UT after PWHT to confirm thickness restoration → Final VT.",
"ASME PCC-2 Article 2.1 weld overlay repair sequence: surface examination (PT/MT) of the base metal before welding to confirm no base metal cracking; VT during inter-pass; surface NDE after final pass to confirm no surface cracks in the overlay; UT after PWHT to verify restored thickness; VT of complete repair. RT is not typically used for overlay repairs because the variable geometry makes interpretation difficult; UT is the preferred volumetric method.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"29. An API 653 tank inspection reveals that the shell-to-bottom weld (annular weld) has failed a vacuum box test — bubbles were observed through the test solution at two locations, each approximately 30 mm long. What is the immediate action?",
"Document the locations and monitor during the next inspection cycle.\nIssue an NCR, drain the tank (or maintain level below the weld leak locations), excavate and repair the failed weld sections, and re-test by vacuum box before return to service.\nSeal the locations with epoxy filler and re-test.\nContinue to fill the tank to the designed operational level; the hydrostatic pressure will seal the minor leaks.",
"Issue an NCR, drain the tank (or maintain level below the weld leak locations), excavate and repair the failed weld sections, and re-test by vacuum box before return to service.",
"Vacuum box test failures (bubble formation) indicate through-weld leaks at the shell-to-bottom joint — a primary containment failure. The tank cannot remain in service with leaking bottom welds. The locations must be marked, the tank drained or level controlled, welds excavated and repaired, and re-tested by vacuum box to confirm repair effectiveness.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"30. During mechanical completion inspection of a pressure vessel, the inspector notices the nameplate is missing from the vessel — it was removed during sandblasting and not re-attached. What is the significance and what must be done before the vessel can be accepted?",
"Minor cosmetic issue — replace the nameplate with the original data.\nSignificant finding — the nameplate is a mandatory regulatory requirement (ASME VIII UG-119); the vessel cannot be put into service without a nameplate or an authorised alternative (stamped data plate); the manufacturer's stamping record must be obtained and a replacement nameplate produced and re-attached per jurisdictional authority requirements.\nAccept — maintain the data sheet in the vessel record file; a nameplate is not required if documentation is available.\nAttach a temporary label with the vessel data and accept for commissioning.",
"Significant finding — the nameplate is a mandatory regulatory requirement (ASME VIII UG-119); the vessel cannot be put into service without a nameplate or an authorised alternative (stamped data plate); the manufacturer's stamping record must be obtained and a replacement nameplate produced and re-attached per jurisdictional authority requirements.",
"ASME VIII UG-119 mandates that every ASME-coded pressure vessel have a code symbol stamp and nameplate permanently attached. Operating a pressure vessel without the required nameplate may violate local pressure vessel regulations (Saudi Standards / SASO). The nameplate must be replaced with the manufacturer's assistance using the original stamping records.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"31. A vibration spectrum analysis of a centrifugal pump shows a dominant frequency at 2× running speed (2X). The pump running speed is 2,950 RPM. What does the 2X peak most commonly indicate?",
"Rotor unbalance — single-plane imbalance always produces a 1X dominant frequency, not 2X.\nMisalignment — angular or parallel misalignment between the pump and motor shaft is the most common cause of a dominant 2X vibration component.\nBearing defect frequency — outer race defect produces sub-harmonic frequencies, not 2X.\nResonance — structural resonance is always at natural frequency, not at 2X running speed.",
"Misalignment — angular or parallel misalignment between the pump and motor shaft is the most common cause of a dominant 2X vibration component.",
"Vibration frequency analysis: 1X = unbalance or bent shaft; 2X = misalignment (particularly angular misalignment, which creates a twice-per-revolution force pattern from coupling flexing); 0.5X = rub or oil whirl; bearing defect frequencies are non-synchronous harmonics calculated from bearing geometry. A dominant 2X component strongly suggests misalignment.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"32. An ASME VIII Div.1 pressure vessel undergoes a repair involving welding on the shell while the vessel is in service (hot tapping). What is the minimum requirement before hot-tapping welding can proceed?",
"The vessel must be completely depressurised before any welding on the shell.\nA hot-tap welding procedure must be developed per ASME PCC-2 Article 2.10 or equivalent, including assessment of hydrogen blistering risk, minimum wall thickness for welding without burn-through, and a written safe work procedure approved by the owner and an authorised pressure vessel inspector.\nHot-tap welding requires only a permit-to-work and fire watch; no formal procedure is needed.\nHot-tap welding is prohibited on ASME VIII vessels — the vessel must be shut down.",
"A hot-tap welding procedure must be developed per ASME PCC-2 Article 2.10 or equivalent, including assessment of hydrogen blistering risk, minimum wall thickness for welding without burn-through, and a written safe work procedure approved by the owner and an authorised pressure vessel inspector.",
"Hot-tap welding on an in-service vessel is one of the highest-risk maintenance activities. ASME PCC-2 Article 2.10 provides requirements: heat calculations to ensure minimum wall thickness against burn-through (typically minimum 6.4 mm for carbon steel), hydrogen blistering risk assessment for H₂ service, HAZ hardness control, PWHT considerations, and formal written hot-tap procedure with owner and inspector approval.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"33. During API 510 inspection, a vertical pressure vessel shows evidence of external CUI (corrosion under insulation) at a pipe support shoe area. The measured thickness at the shoe contact is 7.8 mm (original nominal 12 mm, minimum required 8.5 mm). What is the immediate action?",
"Accept — the measurement is close to minimum and may be within instrument measurement uncertainty of ±0.5 mm.\nIssue an immediate fitness-for-service concern — 7.8 mm is below the 8.5 mm minimum required thickness; the vessel must not be operated at MAWP until an API 579 FFS assessment is completed or the area is repaired.\nAccept with a 6-month monitoring programme.\nRemove the pipe support shoe and expand the measurement grid before making any determination.",
"Issue an immediate fitness-for-service concern — 7.8 mm is below the 8.5 mm minimum required thickness; the vessel must not be operated at MAWP until an API 579 FFS assessment is completed or the area is repaired.",
"7.8 mm < 8.5 mm minimum is a below-minimum-thickness finding with immediate implications. The vessel must either be derated (reduced MAWP) or removed from service pending API 579 FFS assessment or repair. Additional measurements to scope the full extent of the thinning are also required before FFS assessment.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"34. A pressure vessel PWHT record shows the thermocouple attached to a weld reached 750°C for 2 hours. However, examination of the chart shows the thermocouple was insulated and positioned 50 mm away from the weld surface — not directly on the weld metal. What is the implication for PWHT validity?",
"The PWHT is valid — the thermocouple temperature (750°C) demonstrates that the surrounding area achieved the required temperature, and heat conduction ensures the weld reached equivalent temperature.\nThe PWHT validity is questionable — the thermocouple must be in direct contact with the weld or HAZ to confirm weld temperature; a TC positioned 50 mm away in insulation may show significantly different temperature than the actual weld metal; the PWHT may be invalid and must be repeated with correctly positioned thermocouples.\nThe PWHT is valid — the insulation around the TC provides accurate radiant temperature measurement.\nThe PWHT is valid — temperature at 50 mm is within the heat-affected zone which is considered equivalent.",
"The PWHT validity is questionable — the thermocouple must be in direct contact with the weld or HAZ to confirm weld temperature; a TC positioned 50 mm away in insulation may show significantly different temperature than the actual weld metal; the PWHT may be invalid and must be repeated with correctly positioned thermocouples.",
"ASME VIII Appendix 49 and SAES-W-011: thermocouples must be attached directly to the weld or HAZ surface (not embedded in insulation) to monitor actual weld temperature. A TC in insulation 50 mm from the weld can show an artificially elevated or different temperature due to insulation heat storage effects. The PWHT records are invalid and the PWHT must be repeated with correctly placed TCs.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"35. An API 520 back-pressure assessment for a conventional PRV (not balanced bellows) in gas service shows that the total built-up back pressure during relief is 15% of set pressure. The maximum allowed for a conventional PRV is 10% of set pressure. What is the consequence and corrective action?",
"Accept — 15% is close to the 10% limit and within field measurement uncertainty.\nThe conventional PRV is unacceptable for this service — back pressure exceeding 10% of set pressure causes conventional PRVs to either not open at set pressure or to chatter; either replace with a balanced bellows PRV that can tolerate higher back pressure, or redesign the discharge piping to reduce back pressure.\nAccept — the 10% limit applies only to liquid service PRVs, not gas service.\nAccept — back-pressure limits are vendor recommendations, not mandatory API 520 requirements.",
"The conventional PRV is unacceptable for this service — back pressure exceeding 10% of set pressure causes conventional PRVs to either not open at set pressure or to chatter; either replace with a balanced bellows PRV that can tolerate higher back pressure, or redesign the discharge piping to reduce back pressure.",
"API 520 Pt.I Section 5: conventional PRVs are limited to maximum back pressure of 10% of set pressure before performance is significantly affected. Above 10%, the spring-loaded conventional design either has reduced lift (doesn't open fully) or chatters. Balanced bellows PRVs can tolerate up to 50% back pressure. The corrective action is design modification.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"36. During pre-commissioning checks of a fire water pump per SAES-B-017, the diesel engine starting battery shows a capacity test result of 65% of rated capacity. The SAES-B-017 minimum battery capacity for fire pump duty is 80%. What is the finding?",
"Accept — 65% capacity is sufficient for a short-duration fire pump start cycle.\nNCR — battery capacity of 65% is below the 80% minimum required for fire pump duty; the battery must be replaced before the fire water system can be accepted for fire protection duty.\nAccept with a 30-day monitoring plan; battery capacity often recovers after initial charging cycles.\nAccept — fire pump battery requirements apply only to electric-motor-driven pumps, not diesel-driven pumps.",
"NCR — battery capacity of 65% is below the 80% minimum required for fire pump duty; the battery must be replaced before the fire water system can be accepted for fire protection duty.",
"Fire water pump systems are life-safety critical. The diesel engine starting battery must meet the specified minimum capacity to ensure reliable starting in an emergency. SAES-B-017 minimum 80% capacity is a safety requirement — accepting below-specification batteries risks fire pump unavailability during an emergency.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"37. During an API 510 inspection of a process vessel, the inspector observes that the corrosion inhibitor injection quill inside the vessel is missing — it was removed during a previous maintenance and not reinstalled. The injection quill prevents localised corrosion at the inlet nozzle by distributing the inhibitor. What is the finding?",
"Accept — the inhibitor will distribute by diffusion without the quill.\nMajor finding — the missing injection quill may cause accelerated localised corrosion at the nozzle inlet area since the inhibitor cannot be directed to the highest-risk zone; the quill must be reinstalled and the nozzle/inlet area inspected for accelerated corrosion that may have occurred during quill absence.\nMinor finding — document for the next maintenance window.\nAccept — injection quills are optional components and their removal is at the operators' discretion.",
"Major finding — the missing injection quill may cause accelerated localised corrosion at the nozzle inlet area since the inhibitor cannot be directed to the highest-risk zone; the quill must be reinstalled and the nozzle/inlet area inspected for accelerated corrosion that may have occurred during quill absence.",
"Corrosion inhibitor injection quills are design components that protect critical areas from accelerated corrosion. Their absence is an equipment deficiency, not just a minor finding. The nozzle and inlet area must be inspected for corrosion damage that may have occurred during the period of quill absence.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"38. A pressure vessel inspection per API 510 reveals stress corrosion cracking (SCC) in the heat-affected zone of a nozzle weld. The SCC appears as a network of fine cracks (15–20 mm long each) observed in a 300 mm × 200 mm area. The material is 304L SS. What is the most likely cause and the required immediate action?",
"Cause: sigma phase embrittlement from high-temperature service; Action: PWHT and continue operation.\nCause: chloride SCC from external surface contamination (insulation leachate, cooling water ingress) at elevated temperature; Action: immediate FFS assessment, reduce or eliminate the chloride source, assess whether the cracked area can be repaired by weld overlay or must be replaced.\nCause: hydrogen embrittlement from H₂ service; Action: change to duplex SS.\nCause: caustic SCC from process fluid; Action: install corrosion-resistant liner.",
"Cause: chloride SCC from external surface contamination (insulation leachate, cooling water ingress) at elevated temperature; Action: immediate FFS assessment, reduce or eliminate the chloride source, assess whether the cracked area can be repaired by weld overlay or must be replaced.",
"304L SS is very susceptible to chloride SCC at temperatures >60°C. The HAZ is a preferential location due to residual stress. External chloride sources (wet insulation, sea spray, cooling water) are common in petrochemical facilities. The FFS assessment (API 579) must determine whether the cracks are through-wall or benign surface cracks, before repair vs. replacement decision.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"39. During a cooling tower fan gearbox inspection, the inspector finds that the gear tooth contact pattern (blue-dye check) shows 50% tooth contact width and 40% tooth height contact. The specification requires >70% contact width and >55% height contact. What is the finding?",
"Accept — 50% contact width is within the typical ±15% manufacturing tolerance for gearbox tooth contact.\nNCR — the contact pattern does not meet specification; inadequate tooth contact causes stress concentration and accelerated wear; the gearing must be re-adjusted (shimming, re-shimming of gear supports) to achieve specified contact pattern before commissioning.\nAccept — blue-dye contact checks are informational only and do not have binding acceptance criteria.\nAccept — the gearbox will self-adjust during run-in operation.",
"NCR — the contact pattern does not meet specification; inadequate tooth contact causes stress concentration and accelerated wear; the gearing must be re-adjusted (shimming, re-shimming of gear supports) to achieve specified contact pattern before commissioning.",
"Gear tooth contact pattern is a critical acceptance criterion — incorrect contact (too narrow or too concentrated) causes stress concentrations leading to premature gear tooth failure. Specification requirements for contact width and height are binding acceptance criteria. Self-adjustment during run-in is not an accepted disposition for below-specification contact patterns.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"40. An ASME VIII Div.1 pressure vessel is scheduled for replacement of all nozzle gaskets during a turnaround. The maintenance team wants to reuse the spiral-wound gaskets that show no visual damage. What is the correct QC guidance?",
"Approve reuse — if the gaskets show no visual damage, they are acceptable for reuse.\nRecommend against reuse — spiral-wound gaskets are single-use items; once compressed during initial use, the winding cannot return to its original density and sealing capability is reduced; all gaskets should be replaced with new ones.\nApprove reuse — gaskets may be reused up to three times per API 510 guidance.\nApprove reuse for non-critical services; recommend replacement for high-pressure services only.",
"Recommend against reuse — spiral-wound gaskets are single-use items; once compressed during initial use, the winding cannot return to its original density and sealing capability is reduced; all gaskets should be replaced with new ones.",
"Spiral-wound gaskets are compressed to their design seating stress during initial assembly. The wound metal seating wire takes a permanent set and cannot re-expand to original geometry. Reused gaskets provide reduced seating stress and increased leak risk — particularly problematic on ASME VIII pressure vessels. Industry practice (ASME PCC-1) and API guidance recommend replacing all gaskets during turnarounds.",
1),

